```apex
/**
 * Synchronises newly created Accounts to the ERP as customers.
 * One HTTP callout per Account so the ERP team can trace each record independently.
 *
 * Callouts are executed asynchronously via @future(callout=true) to:
 *   – stay within the 100-callout-per-transaction governor limit when the
 *     marketplace integration inserts batches of up to 200 accounts, and
 *   – avoid blocking the user / integration DML transaction.
 *
 * Accounts are chunked into groups of ≤ 100 before handing them to a future
 * method, so a 200-account insert spawns two futures, each performing 100
 * individual callouts.
 */
public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync {

    private static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 100;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------ public
    /**
     * Entry point called from the trigger.
     * Splits Trigger.new into safe chunks and dispatches async callout work.
     */
    public static void process(List<Account> accounts) {
        if (accounts == null || accounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Slice into chunks that respect the per-transaction callout limit.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < accounts.size(); i += CHUNK_SIZE) {
            Integer end = Math.min(i + CHUNK_SIZE, accounts.size());
            List<Account> chunk = accounts.subList(i, end);

            // Dispatch each chunk asynchronously.
            syncChunk(chunk);
        }
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------- internal async
    @future(callout=true)
    private static void syncChunk(List<Account> accounts) {
        Http http = new Http();

        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
            req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
            req.setMethod('POST');
            req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
            req.setBody(buildPayload(acc));
            req.setTimeout(120000); // 2 min – generous; ERP is async (202).

            http.send(req);
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ payload build
    /**
     * Produces the JSON body for a single-account request.
     * Example: [{"salesforceId":"001...","name":"Acme GmbH","country":null}]
     */
    private static String buildPayload(Account acc) {
        String country = String.isBlank(acc.BillingCountry)
            ? null
            : acc.BillingCountry;

        List<Map<String, Object>> body = new List<Map<String, Object>>{
            new Map<String, Object>{
                'salesforceId' => acc.Id,
                'name'         => acc.Name,
                'country'      => country   // null serialises as JSON null
            }
        };

        return JSON.serialize(body);
    }
}
```

---

### Why this shape

| Concern | How it's addressed |
|---|---|
| One HTTP request per Account | The `for` loop in `syncChunk` issues exactly one `http.send` per record. |
| 200-account batch & 100-callout limit | `process` slices into ≤ 100-per-chunk and fires one `@future` per slice; each future gets a fresh 100-callout budget. |
| Callout context restrictions (e.g., trigger fired inside an already-callout'd transaction) | Async `@future` runs in its own transaction, so the caller's callout history doesn't matter. |
| JSON escaping / null country | `JSON.serialize` handles special characters in names/countries; a blank `BillingCountry` is mapped to `null` per the spec. |
| Trigger stays thin | The trigger is one guarded call into `ErpCustomerSync.process`; all logic lives in the testable class. |

## What I did differently, and why

**Per-record inserts in a loop cannot handle 250 records.** The DML governor limit is 150 statements per transaction. 250 individual `insert` calls would throw a `LimitException` and roll back the *entire* transaction, defeating the requirement that one failure doesn't block the rest.

The idiomatic Salesforce equivalent of "insert each one independently, but don't let a rejection stop the others" is a single `Database.insert(tasks, false)` call. With `allOrNone = false`, validation-rule rejections come back as `isSuccess() == false` entries on the `SaveResult` array—no exception, no rollback, remaining records still commit. It's one DML statement instead of 250, and the per-record error mapping is still straightforward.

I also used a single SOQL query for all opportunities (one query, not N queries inside a loop) and a `with sharing` declaration so the service respects the running user's record-level access.

```apex
// File: force-app/main/default/classes/FollowUpTaskService.cls

/**
 * Service that creates a follow-up Task for each Opportunity passed in.
 *
 * Design note – why Database.insert(list, false) instead of per-record try/catch:
 *   The weekly pipeline review can surface 250+ opportunities in a single call.
 *   Individual `insert` inside a loop would issue one DML statement per record
 *   and blow past the 150-DML-per-transaction governor limit.  Database.insert
 *   with allOrNone = false collapses everything into ONE DML call while still
 *   isolating validation-rule failures: rejected Tasks simply appear as
 *   SaveResult errors and never prevent the remaining Tasks from being saved.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates a "Follow up" Task for every Opportunity in the supplied list.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds  Ids of the Opportunities to follow up on.
     * @return  Set of Opportunity Ids whose Task could NOT be created
     *          (validation failure, missing record, etc.).  Empty when every
     *          Task was inserted successfully.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // --- 1 SOQL query regardless of list size ---
        List<Opportunity> opps = [
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        // If any Ids didn't resolve (deleted / inaccessible), treat them as
        // failed so the caller knows those follow-ups were never created.
        Set<Id> foundOppIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : opps) {
            foundOppIds.add(opp.Id);
        }
        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            if (!foundOppIds.contains(oppId)) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(oppId);
            }
        }

        // --- Build all Tasks in memory ---
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        // Parallel list so we can map each SaveResult back to its Opportunity.
        List<Id> taskToOppId = new List<Id>();

        for (Opportunity opp : opps) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = opp.Id,
                OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                Status       = 'Not Started',
                Priority     = 'Normal'
            ));
            taskToOppId.add(opp.Id);
        }

        if (tasks.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // --- ONE DML call; allOrNone = false keeps going on validation errors ---
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);

        // --- Collect failures ---
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(taskToOppId[i]);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

