## Design decisions & explanation

**Why I batch Queueable jobs into groups of 50 instead of strictly one-per-case:**
Salesforce enforces a hard limit of **50 concurrent Queueable jobs** in the Flex Queue. The nightly API job can close 200 cases in a single transaction; a naive loop of `enqueueJob` per case would throw an `AsyncException` and fail the entire DML. I chunk the qualifying case IDs into groups of ≤ 50 so we stay within the limit. Each Queueable still processes its assigned cases **independently** (per-case `try/catch`), so a failure on one case never aborts the others—satisfying the isolation requirement while remaining bulk-safe.

**Why the Queueable re-queries the Case:**
By the time the async job executes, the record state may have shifted (e.g., a parallel integration re-opened the case). Re-querying with a guard (`IsClosed == true`) keeps the logic correct without relying on trigger context that no longer exists.

**Why `Database.insert(tasks, false)`:**
A single bulk DML with `allOrNone = false` means one bad Task row doesn't prevent the rest from committing. It also keeps us at one DML call per Queueable execution (the limit is 150 per transaction, but fewer calls is always better, and this pattern extends cleanly to the planned vendor callout).

