/**
 * Handler for AccountTrigger (after update).
 *
 * When an account's BillingCountry changes, push the new country to its
 * contacts whose MailingCountry is blank / null or still equals the
 * account's *previous* BillingCountry.  Contacts that already have a
 * different MailingCountry (they work abroad) are left alone.
 *
 * Bulk-safe: one filtered SOQL + one DML regardless of trigger size.
 */
public with sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    public void handleAfterUpdate(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldMap) {

        // 1. Identify accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed
        //    (skip rows where it went from one value to null – that is
        //     usually a data-cleanup edit, not a HQ move).
        List<Account> changedAccounts = new List<Account>();
        for (Account acc : newAccounts) {
            String oldCountry = oldMap.get(acc.Id).BillingCountry;
            String newCountry = acc.BillingCountry;

            if (newCountry != null && newCountry != oldCountry) {
                changedAccounts.add(acc);
            }
        }

        if (changedAccounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. Single, *scoped* query – only contacts under the accounts
        //    whose country changed.  Still one SOQL, still no loop.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Account acc : changedAccounts) {
            accountIds.add(acc.Id);
        }

        List<Contact> candidates = [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
        ];

        if (candidates.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 3. Fast lookup tables keyed by AccountId
        Map<Id, String> newCountryByAccount = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> oldCountryByAccount = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Account acc : changedAccounts) {
            newCountryByAccount.put(acc.Id, acc.BillingCountry);
            oldCountryByAccount.put(acc.Id, oldMap.get(acc.Id).BillingCountry);
        }

        // 4. Filter: blank / null MailingCountry, or equal to previous country
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : candidates) {
            String previous = oldCountryByAccount.get(c.AccountId);

            if (String.isBlank(c.MailingCountry) || c.MailingCountry == previous) {
                c.MailingCountry = newCountryByAccount.get(c.AccountId);
                toUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }

        // 5. Single bulk DML
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

### Why this still satisfies "one query, no loops"

| Your original idea | This version |
|---|---|
| `SELECT … FROM Contact` → 2.3 M rows → **governor-limit crash** | `SELECT … FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :ids` → bounded to the ≤ 200 changed accounts |
| One query | One query ✓ |
| No queries in loops | No queries in loops ✓ |
| Load into a map, pick what you need | Build lookup maps from the result, pick what you need ✓ |

If you ever scale past 50 000 contacts under a single batch of 200 accounts, move the heavy path into a **Queueable / Batch** class that pages with `Database.QueryLocator` — but for a typical MDM window the filtered query above will stay well within limits.

